Security Principles
Principle 1 - Resources Only
The server exposes MCP Resources only.
Allowed:
	• confluence://spaces
	• confluence://pages
	• confluence://page/{id}
	• confluence://search
Not Allowed:
	• MCP Tools

Principle 2 - Least Privilege
The MCP server must never return information that the user cannot access in Confluence.
Requirements:
	• User identity propagated to Confluence
	• Authorization decision made by Confluence
	• Audit trail maintained
	• Server permissions narrower than source permissions if required

Principle 3 - Trust Boundary Enforcement
All inbound parameters are untrusted.
Requirements:
	• Strict Pydantic validation
	• Strong typing
	• No dynamic query construction
	• Parameterized queries only
	• Output validation
	• Output sanitization

Principle 4 - Secrets Management
Never store:
	• Passwords
	• Tokens
	• API Keys
	• Certificates
inside source code.
Secrets must be obtained from:
Azure Key Vault
